DISK MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
**********************************************************
1. Check the disk usage :

Shows the filesystem usage and avaliable spaces

 Command : df -h


2. Check the Directory Size

 Shows the total Directory size

 Command : du -sh

 Example : du -sh /var/log


3.View BLock Devices

 List all disk and paritions

  Command : lsblk

  Command : lsblk -f 

  -f is shows the additional shows the filesystem information 

4. View Physical Volume

  Command : pvs

  pvs shows the physical storage supplied to LVM.

  PFree = 0 : It means all physical volume [Eg:19GB] has been allocated to logical volumes


5. Create a physical Volume

  Command : pvcreate /dev/nvme1n1p1

  verify : pvs
   

5. View the Volume Groups

  Command : vgs

  vgs shows the storage pool formed from physical volumes

  it displays :

  -> Volume Group Name 
  ->Number of Pysical Volumes
  ->Number of Logical Volumes
  ->Total Voulme Group Size
  ->Free space available for creating or extending Logical Volumes

  attr = wz--n : It means Volume Groups is writable and active

  VFree = 0 : It means No free space remains in the Volume Group


 6. Show Logical Volumes
 
  Command : lvs

  lvs shows the usable logical disks created from the volume group.

  it shows the logical volumes , volume groups and Size

  if Attr = -wi-ao---- : 

  w — Logical volume is writable.
  i — Uses normal linear allocation.
  a — Logical volume is active.
  o — Logical volume is open and currently in use. 

7. Check the Swap is active

  Command : swapon --show

  Eg : Swap :2:GB


8. Disable Swap Temporarly

 Command : swapoff -a

 Verify : 

 free -h (or) swapon --show


9. Enable Swap Again

  Command : swapon -a

  or 

  Command : swapon /dev/rl/swap 


10.Display Partition Information

 Displays detailed information about all disks and their partitions.

  Command : fdisk -l

11.Enter the fdisk Interactive mode

  command : fdisk /dev/nvme0n1

  types :

   p : Command prints the current partition table

   n : Create New partition

   d : Delete an existing partitions

   l : List partition types

   w : save changes

   F : this command is display the free unpartitioned space on the disk

12. Make File System

  A partition by itself is just allocated storage. To store normal files and directories on it, we create a filesystem.

 Disk
  ↓
Partition
  ↓
Filesystem
  ↓
Mount Point
  ↓
Files & Directories

 Example : mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme0n1p3

13. Attaches a filesystem to a directory

  command : mount

  The mount command attaches a filesystem to a directory so that its storage can be accessed through that directory.

   Example : mount /dev/nvme0n1p3 /data
              |          |           |  
            command    filesystem  mount-point


12. Identify block devices

   command : blkid 
     
    blkid displays information about block devices, especially the UUID and filesystem type.

    UUID -> Unique Identifier of the file system

    TYPE -> FileSystem type (xfs)

    PARTUUID -> Unique Identifier of the Partiton


13. Detach a filesystem

   command : umount

   Example : umount /dev/nvme0n1p3


14.Check mounted filesystems

 Shows which disks are currently attached (mounted) to which directories

 command : findmnt

 findmnt : Displays all currently mounted filesystem in a tree structure

 it shows : where the filesystem mounted (TARGET), which device it comes from (SOURCE), what filesystem type it uses(FSTYPE), mount option(OPTIONS)

  TARGET  → /data
  SOURCE  → /dev/nvme0n1p3
  FSTYPE  → xfs
  OPTIONS → rw...

